1) Compute a paint key per element. Add it in walk() right after `cs` is known:
```js
function paintKey(el, cs) {
  const pcs = el.parentElement ? css(el.parentElement) : null;
  const positioned = cs.position !== 'static';
  const flexItem = !!pcs && /flex|grid/.test(pcs.display);
  const zApplies = (positioned || flexItem) && cs.zIndex !== 'auto';
  const z = zApplies ? (parseInt(cs.zIndex, 10) || 0) : 0;
  if (zApplies && z < 0) return { b: 0, z };
  if (zApplies && z > 0) return { b: 3, z };
  // These paint in the positioned z:auto/0 layer even when static.
  const pseudoPos = positioned || zApplies || parseFloat(cs.opacity) < 1 || cs.transform !== 'none' ||
    cs.filter !== 'none' || cs.isolation === 'isolate' || (cs.mixBlendMode && cs.mixBlendMode !== 'normal');
  return { b: pseudoPos ? 2 : 1, z: 0 };
}
```
- In walk(), compute `const pk = paintKey(el, cs)` and set `node._pk = pk` on every node the function returns: leaf(), the text-block path and frameNode.
- For lifted children:
  - In the `plain && kids.length === 1` path and the collapsed-wrapper path, overwrite `k._pk = pk` when `pk.b !== 1`, i.e. when the wrapper itself is positioned or creates a stacking layer. Otherwise keep the child's own key.
  - For `display:contents`, keep the children's own keys.
- looseText() nodes get `{ b: 1, z: 0 }`.
- Every node also needs a DOM sequence: in childrenOf(), stamp `n._i = idx++` on each pushed node.

2) Order the children of each frame AFTER tryAutoLayout(), so Auto Layout detection still sees DOM order.
```js
function cmpPaint(a, b) {
  const A = a._pk || { b: 1, z: 0 }, B = b._pk || { b: 1, z: 0 };
  return (A.b - B.b) || (A.z - B.z) || (a._i - b._i);
}
function stackOrder(frame) {
  const kids = frame.children;
  if (!kids || kids.length < 2) return;
  const sorted = kids.slice().sort(cmpPaint);
  if (frame.layout) {
    // Auto Layout flow order = layer order of the non-absolute kids. They don't overlap
    // (fitLayout checked it), so keep their DOM order and only move absolute kids.
    const flow = kids.filter((k) => !k.absolute);
    let fi = 0;
    frame.children = sorted.map((k) => (k.absolute ? k : flow[fi++]));
  } else {
    frame.children = sorted;
  }
}
```
- Call stackOrder(f) right after every `tryAutoLayout(f, cs)` in walk(), and stackOrder(root) after `tryAutoLayout(root, bodyCS)` in extract().
- Finally, strip `_pk` and `_i` recursively before posting the tree, or ignore them, since code.js does not read unknown keys.
- No code.js changes are needed. ABSOLUTE children may sit anywhere in an Auto Layout frame's layer list without affecting flow, and buildNode() already appends in array order.

